  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Mild conditions the rest of the week with only minor\n  disturbances. \n\n- Shortwave Monday to bring breezy conditions.\n\n- Potential for a cold front Friday into the weekend, but\n  confidence is low. \n\n- Chances are increasing for a warm, dry early October beyond the\n  7 day forecast. \n\nOverall mild conditions are expected the rest of the week. The\nweather patterns we expect throughout the week are: weak zonal\n(west-to-east) flow aloft, a shortwave on Monday in split flow,\nand some weak ridging. The shortwave on Monday will cause some\nbreezy conditions, but it is a result of a cutoff low splitting\nout of the flow and diving into the U.S. Southwest. As a result,\nmost of the energy and moisture will go to the south. During\nperiods of zonal flow, there will be some moisture mixed in, but\nit will only affect far northwest Montana and Glacier National\nPark around Tuesday and Wednesday. Even for that though there's\nonly a 40% chance of up to 0.10 inches of precipitation in the\nmountains. \n\nSome models, namely the GFS, are gearing up for another cold front\nto end the week on Friday into Saturday. Looking at ensemble\nclusters for rain, current forecasts only show an 11% chance of\nsome moisture with that event again in northwest Montana. Most\nensemble members don't show a cold front at all. \n\nActive weather in the tropics right now is setting up the pattern\nin the long term. With 3 hurricanes in the central and east\nPacific Ocean from Hawaii to Baja and a typhoon approaching the\neastern periphery of Japan, it's been a difficult forecast for\nmodels. However, they're starting to coalesce around higher\nchances for a potent ridge over the northern Rockies into the\nfirst third of October. Ensemble mean temperature forecasts are\ncoming in 15 degrees above seasonal averages at about 9-12 days\nout. Those are some large anomalies for that long of a lead time!\nAdd in that the signal is coming from multiple ensemble systems\nincluding both dynamical (physics based) and AI modeling systems,\nit gives above normal levels of confidence for such a long lead\ntime forecast. Looking back, the last time this kind of warm \nridge set up over the northwestern US in early October was october\n9-11 and 14, 1991. Eureka, Montana hit 86 degrees, Grangeville, \nIdaho 87 degrees, and Butte 81 degrees! The Center for Climate \nPrediction has issued forecasts for the northern Rockies region \nwith a 60-70% chance of above average temperatures and \nprecipitation forecasts are leaning below average for that time \nperiod as well.",
